---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Batteries in Series vs Parallel: Voltage and Capacity Wiring';
const description = 'Series wiring adds voltage (two 12V batteries make 24V); parallel wiring adds capacity (two 100Ah batteries make 200Ah). The two rules and the matching caveats.';
const canonical = '/technology/series-vs-parallel/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Batteries in series vs parallel',
  articleSection: 'Battery Technology',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between series and parallel batteries?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Series adds voltage: two 12V batteries wired in series make 24V at the same capacity. Parallel adds capacity: two 100Ah batteries wired in parallel make 200Ah at the same voltage. Series changes the voltage; parallel changes the amp-hours.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can you mix old and new batteries in a bank?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Not advised. A bank is limited by its weakest battery — a new battery paired with a worn one is dragged down by it, and in series the imbalance causes one battery to overcharge. Banks should use matched batteries of the same type, age and capacity.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Technology · Fundamentals · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Batteries in Series vs Parallel: Voltage and Capacity Wiring</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two wiring patterns answer almost every multi-battery question, and they do opposite things: one raises
        the voltage, the other raises the capacity.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>Series</strong> wiring (positive to negative) <strong>adds
        voltage</strong> — two 12V batteries make 24V at the same amp-hours. <strong>Parallel</strong> wiring
        (positive to positive, negative to negative) <strong>adds capacity</strong> — two 100Ah batteries make
        200Ah at the same voltage. Series changes the voltage; parallel changes the capacity.
      </div>

      <h2>The two rules</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Wiring</th><th>Result</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Series</strong></td><td>Voltage adds, capacity stays</td><td>2 × 12V 100Ah → 24V 100Ah</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Parallel</strong></td><td>Capacity adds, voltage stays</td><td>2 × 12V 100Ah → 12V 200Ah</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Series serves higher-voltage systems (24V trucks, 48V banks); parallel serves longer runtime at the same
        voltage. Combined series-parallel banks serve both at once — the building block of every large
        installation.
      </p>

      <h2>The matching rule</h2>
      <p>
        A bank is only as good as its <strong>weakest battery</strong>, so batteries in a bank should be
        <strong>matched</strong> — same chemistry, type, capacity and age. In series, an old battery's higher
        resistance forces it to overcharge while the others lag; in parallel, a weak battery drags the healthy ones
        down. Mixing old and new is the most common bank-killing mistake.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My position:</strong> The series-vs-parallel rules are two sentences — voltage adds in series,
          capacity adds in parallel — but the real lesson is the matching rule, because a bank fails at its weakest
          battery, not its average.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Supporting logic:</strong> Wiring mistakes are rare; mismatch mistakes are everywhere. Once you know the two
          rules and that banks must be matched, you can build and diagnose any multi-battery system — and avoid the
          old-plus-new pairing that quietly ruins most banks.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and system documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery engineering references — series and parallel bank design.</li>
        <li>System documentation — bank wiring and matching guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
